+Notes, interpretation & where Aegis differs:
+- Sigstore (cosign + Rekor) is the canonical open standard for signing + transparency logs; Aegis builds on it and integrates those logs into a searchable Model Context Protocol (MCP) — giving a single searchable index that ties together CI, MLflow, HIL receipts, deployments and federated rounds. That is the major differentiator.
+- Major cloud vendors (AWS/GCP/Azure) provide strong registry, serving and KMS primitives, but they do not provide Rekor-style transparency logs, non‑repudiable HIL receipt patterns, or an MCP abstraction out of the box. Aegis bundles these systems together and supplies automation, runbooks and edge/OTA safety patterns that enterprise safety‑critical users need.
+- Databricks and some MLOps vendors focus on model lifecycle and governance inside their stack; Aegis intentionally remains vendor‑agnostic and acts as the cross‑cutting control plane that can integrate with MLflow, SageMaker, Vertex, etc.
+- Model governance startups provide some approvals & policy features, but they rarely include supply‑chain transparency, HSM/BYOK + Rekor attestation integration, or federated/DP scaffolding.
+
+Recommended positioning from matrix:
+- For enterprises that require auditability/regulatory evidence (healthcare, automotive, aerospace, defense), position Aegis as the missing link: "single source of provable truth for models across CI, MLflow, HIL, OTA and federated learning."
+- For organizations that already use cloud vendor stacks, emphasize Aegis as a neutral governance layer (MCP + Rekor + HIL) that augments existing registries rather than replacing them.
